<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dungannon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dungannon is a town in County Tyrone, Northern Ireland. It was the chief seat of the O'Neill kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tir-eoghain">Tír Eoghain</a> from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, with their inauguration site at nearby Tullyhogue, and Hugh O'Neill ruled from its castle. In 1782 a convention of the Irish Volunteers there demanded legislative independence for Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dungannon.jpg" alt="Dungannon" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Linda Bailey · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_St_Patrick,_Dungannon_-_geograph.org.uk_-_112576.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For centuries Dungannon's fortunes were tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-neill-dynasty">O'Neill dynasty</a>, which ruled a large part of Ulster until the 17th century. The O'Neills were traditionally inaugurated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tullyhogue-fort">Tullyhogue Fort</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> mound some four miles north-east of the town, where the O'Hagan clan were stewards. In the 14th century they built a castle on Castle Hill. Hugh O'Neill, 2nd Earl of Tyrone, burned it in 1602 as Crown forces under Lord Mountjoy closed in near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Nine Years' War</a>. After the Flight of the Earls in 1607, the lands were confiscated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a>, and Dungannon and its castle were granted to Sir Arthur Chichester.</p><p>Sir Phelim O'Neill seized the town at the start of the Irish Rebellion of 1641 and issued the Proclamation of Dungannon, setting out the rebels' aims and professing loyalty to Charles I. The town changed hands several times in the Irish Confederate Wars, and in 1689 a Jacobite force occupied Castle Hill, which hosted King James II on his way to the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a>.</p><p>In 1782, as the most central town of Ulster, Dungannon was chosen for a convention of the Volunteers, who had formed for defence against the French in the American War of Independence. Delegates from 147 corps met at the Presbyterian church on Scotch Street and resolved that the British Crown's claim to overrule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> was unconstitutional and illegal. Further conventions followed in 1783 and 1793, when the Volunteers divided over Catholic emancipation and parliamentary reform. Local veterans split between the Orangemen and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a>, and martial law from January 1797 broke the local United Irish organisation.</p><p>The town became a linen centre; by 1802 a surveyor described it as one of the most prosperous in the north of Ireland in the linen trade. A workhouse opened in 1842 and closed in 1948, and a memorial commemorates those sheltered there, including victims of the Great Famine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dungannon had 16,282 people at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and is the second-largest town in the historic County Tyrone. In 1963 protests against housing allocation began a campaign that led to Northern Ireland's first civil rights march, from Coalisland to Dungannon, on 24 August 1968. During the Troubles almost 50 people were killed in and around the town. The poultry processor Moy Park, founded in 1943 in the neighbouring village of Moygashel, is the largest employer, and since the 1990s the food industry has drawn workers from abroad, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. Since 2015 the town has shared local government with Magherafelt and Cookstown in the Mid-Ulster District Council.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dungannon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the late 12th century the O'Neills contested the kingship with the Mac Lochlainn line, which they finally overthrew in 1241, ruling from <b>Dungannon</b> with inaugurations at Tullyhogue.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tir-eoghain">Kingdom of Tir Eoghain</a></li></ul></section>
